(function () {
window.AINOW = window.AINOW || {};
var __exports = AINOW['data/data-journalism'] = AINOW['data/data-journalism'] || {};
const journalismPrompts = [

    {"id":"jou-1","title":"Investigative Story Structure","prompt":"You are a senior investigative journalist with 20 years of experience at ProPublica and The Guardian. I am investigating potential wage theft at a large regional logistics company with 8,000 warehouse workers. I have obtained payroll records showing systematic rounding-down of hours, interviews with 12 workers, and an internal HR memo acknowledging the discrepancy. Help me structure this investigation into a publishable longform piece. Provide: a recommended narrative structure (chronological vs. revelatory vs. character-driven) with justification for this specific story, a story outline with section headers and the key fact or quote that should anchor each section, a list of 8 additional sources I should pursue before publishing (regulatory bodies, labor economists, legal experts, comparable cases), 5 specific records requests I should file under FOIA or state equivalents, and the legal review checklist I should walk through with our publication's attorney before running the story.","tags":["journalism","investigative","longform","structure"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"jou-2","title":"Hard News Lede Workshop","prompt":"You are a news writing coach at a major metropolitan daily. Walk me through the craft of writing a hard news lede from first principles. Explain: what information belongs in the first sentence versus the second, the difference between a summary lede and a delayed lede and when each is appropriate, the five most common mistakes new reporters make in their opening sentences and how to fix each one, how to test whether a lede is working by reading it in isolation, and how to calibrate specificity — when 'a 47-year-old teacher' is better than 'a teacher' and why vagueness undermines credibility. Then write three contrasting ledes for this scenario: a city council voted 5-4 last night to close three public libraries due to a budget shortfall, affecting 200,000 card holders and 45 staff members.","tags":["news-writing","craft","editing","ledes"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-3","title":"Breaking News Triage Protocol","prompt":"You are the night editor at a regional news website that covers a metro area of 1.2 million people. It is 9:47 PM. Your police scanner is reporting a large fire at a downtown apartment building with multiple injuries. You have one reporter available, a smartphone, and a Twitter/X account with 80,000 followers. Walk me through the decision framework for the next 90 minutes: what to publish immediately versus what to hold until verified, how to handle unconfirmed casualty numbers from social media, the exact language to use when reporting information that is unverified but newsworthy, how to update a developing story without contradicting earlier accurate reporting, the three calls your reporter must make before leaving for the scene, and how to communicate uncertainty to readers without undermining their confidence in your coverage.","tags":["breaking-news","editing","ethics","digital"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-4","title":"Nut Graf Master Class","prompt":"You are a features editor who has coached hundreds of writers on story structure. Teach me everything about the nut graf: its function as the contract with the reader, the difference between a nut graf and a thesis statement, how to write a nut graf that answers 'so what' without being preachy or obvious, the ideal length and placement (and why some editors say it should appear no later than the fifth paragraph), and how to revise a weak nut graf without dismantling the entire story. Then write three versions of a nut graf — weak, adequate, and excellent — for this story: A 78-year-old retired postal worker in rural Montana has been teaching free coding classes out of his garage for six years and has placed 34 students in tech jobs paying over $60,000 a year.","tags":["feature-writing","structure","craft","editing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-5","title":"Broadcast News Script Converter","prompt":"You are a television news producer at a network affiliate. I have a print news story that needs to be rewritten for a 90-second broadcast package. The rules of broadcast writing are strict: sentences must be short and spoken aloud naturally, no passive voice, numbers must be written out phonetically, attribution comes before the claim (not after), and each sentence should carry only one idea. Take this scenario and write a broadcast-ready script: The state health department released data today showing that infant mortality in the state's three poorest counties has risen 22 percent over the past five years, with researchers blaming cuts to prenatal care programs and a shortage of OB/GYN physicians. The script should include anchor intro (15 seconds), reporter track (60 seconds), one soundbite placeholder with suggested question, and a tag line for the anchor.","tags":["broadcast","writing","television","script"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-6","title":"News Story Anatomy Audit","prompt":"[Paste your draft article or notes]\n\nYou are a senior copy editor at a national newspaper. Conduct a full structural and factual audit of the draft above. Your review must address: whether the lede earns its place or buries the real news, whether the nut graf is present and functional, whether the story follows a logical structure or wanders, whether all factual claims are attributed to a named or identifiable source, any sentences that could expose the publication to a libel claim and why, paragraphs that can be cut without losing information, the single weakest quote and why it should either be strengthened or replaced, and whether the ending resolves the story or simply stops. Provide specific line edits or rewrites for the three most critical problems you find.","tags":["editing","structure","audit","news-writing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-7","title":"AP Style Enforcer","prompt":"You are an AP Style authority and copy editor. I will describe a common newsroom scenario and you will explain the correct AP Style ruling and the underlying logic. Cover all of the following in one structured guide: when to spell out numbers versus use numerals (and all the exceptions), how to handle titles before and after names, the rules for state abbreviations in datelines versus running text, how to handle percentages and dollar amounts, when 'more than' differs from 'over', how to refer to ages, the correct handling of courtesy titles (Mr., Ms., Dr.) in different publication types, and the three AP Style rules that most commonly trip up reporters covering government and politics. For each rule, give an example of the wrong version and the corrected version.","tags":["ap-style","copy-editing","standards","craft"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-8","title":"Lead Paragraph Variety Pack","prompt":"You are a writing coach who trains journalists at a mid-size regional daily. Take the following story premise and write six structurally distinct opening paragraphs, each representing a different lede technique: (1) the hard summary lede, (2) the anecdotal or scene-setting lede, (3) the delayed lede with a turn, (4) the question lede, (5) the contrast lede that juxtaposes two opposing realities, and (6) the second-day lede that advances a story already in the news. Story premise: The city's largest homeless shelter announced today it is closing in 60 days due to the loss of a federal grant, affecting 340 people currently sleeping there and leaving service providers scrambling. After writing all six, explain which you would choose for a morning newspaper front page and which you would choose for a long-form digital piece, and why.","tags":["news-writing","ledes","craft","structure"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:35:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"jou-9","title":"Advance Story Planning Framework","prompt":"[Describe your upcoming story assignment, civic event, or news subject]\n\nYou are an assignment editor at a regional news outlet. Based on the story or event described above, build a complete advance story plan that includes: the key questions this story must answer for a general reader, the documents I should request in the next 48 hours, the five most important people to interview and what each one uniquely provides, the data I should gather and analyze before coverage begins, two potential angles for a pre-event feature that humanizes the story, the questions most likely to generate newsworthy moments, and the headline and lede I expect to write for each of the two or three most likely outcomes.","tags":["planning","assignment","reporting","news-writing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-10","title":"Second-Day Story Engine","prompt":"[Paste the original news story or describe the breaking news event that was covered yesterday]\n\nYou are a metro editor who excels at finding the second-day angle — the story that follows a breaking news event and goes deeper. Based on the story above, generate 8 distinct second-day story ideas ranging from data-driven approaches to human-interest angles to investigative threads. For each idea, identify the single best source to call first, the one document I should request immediately, and a proposed headline. Then rank the 8 ideas by newsworthiness and explain your top choice. Also flag any angle in the original story that was underreported or ignored entirely.","tags":["news-writing","enterprise","planning","second-day"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-11","title":"FOIA Request Architect","prompt":"[Describe the government agency, topic, and the documents or data you believe exist]\n\nYou are an investigative journalist and freedom of information specialist with expertise in federal and state public records law. Based on the investigation described above, help me build a comprehensive records request strategy. Write a model FOIA/public records request letter that: uses legally precise language, is specific enough to be granted but broad enough to be useful, preemptively addresses the four most common grounds for denial (privacy, law enforcement exemption, deliberative process, burden), includes a fee waiver argument grounded in public interest, and specifies a reasonable timeline. Also list six additional document types or databases I should request from related agencies and explain what each would reveal.","tags":["investigative","foia","legal","records"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-12","title":"Follow the Money: Corporate Investigation","prompt":"You are a financial investigative journalist who has exposed corporate fraud at major publications. I am investigating a mid-size regional hospital network that appears to have funneled money to a construction company owned by a board member's family over a six-year period, with contracts totaling roughly $14 million. I have the hospital's IRS Form 990s, board meeting minutes obtained through a public records request, and the secretary of state filings showing the ownership of the construction company. Walk me through: how to read the Form 990 for red flags in related-party transactions, what the board minutes should reveal if proper conflict-of-interest procedures were followed, how to document the beneficial ownership chain through LLC filings across multiple states, which federal or state agencies might have oversight and whether they have enforcement records, the five experts I need to interview to understand whether this constitutes fraud, self-dealing, or legal if unusual practice, and how to present this material to the hospital for right-of-reply.","tags":["investigative","finance","nonprofits","corporate"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:55:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"jou-13","title":"Whistleblower Protocol","prompt":"You are an investigative reporter with deep expertise in source protection. A government employee has reached out through an anonymous email to say they have documentation of a senior official falsifying environmental inspection reports at a petrochemical facility near a residential neighborhood. The source is frightened. Walk me through the complete whistleblower handling protocol: how to establish a secure communication channel from first contact through publication, what to tell the source about their legal protections and risks under federal and state whistleblower statutes, how to authenticate documents provided by a source who must remain anonymous, the ethical obligations I have to this source including what I owe them if the story leads to their identification, how to independently corroborate the core allegations without relying solely on the documents, and the editorial and legal sign-off process my organization should complete before publishing. Be specific about tools, laws, and procedures.","tags":["investigative","source-protection","whistleblower","ethics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-14","title":"Document Analysis Framework","prompt":"[Paste your draft article or notes]\n\nYou are an investigative journalist and document analyst. I have obtained a large set of records related to my investigation — described or pasted above. Help me build a systematic document analysis framework. Provide: a taxonomy for categorizing the documents by type, relevance, and reliability, a method for identifying internal contradictions across documents that may signal deception or cover-up, the specific language patterns in internal memos and emails that experienced investigators look for as indicators of knowledge or intent, how to handle documents that have been partially redacted and what the redacted portions might contain based on surrounding context, a matrix for mapping each key claim in my story to its primary and secondary documentary support, and a checklist of what I still need to obtain to achieve publishable evidentiary standards for an investigative piece.","tags":["investigative","documents","analysis","research"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-15","title":"Investigative Timeline Reconstruction","prompt":"[Paste your raw chronological notes, document dates, or event log — even if incomplete or disordered]\n\nYou are a senior investigative editor. Using the raw material above, help me reconstruct an authoritative investigative timeline. Your output should: organize all events in precise chronological order and flag where gaps exist, identify the specific moments of decision-making that matter most to the story's accountability dimension, surface any contradictions between dates or events that may signal concealment, suggest the questions I should ask officials to fill the most critical gaps without revealing my full hand, explain how to display this timeline compellingly for general readers, and identify the three or four moments in the timeline that will anchor the story's most important revelations.","tags":["investigative","timeline","editing","structure"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-16","title":"Source Network Mapping","prompt":"You are an investigative reporter covering a story about systemic corruption in a regional transit authority. You suspect bid rigging on construction contracts but have limited initial sourcing. Teach me how to build a source network from scratch for a complex institutional investigation. Explain: how to identify and approach former employees who may have witnessed misconduct, how to find disgruntled vendors or contractors who lost bids, how to use LinkedIn, court filings, and regulatory databases to map key relationships before making your first call, the ethics and strategy of using one source to get to the next without burning either, how to manage a source who wants to provide information but fears retaliation, the difference between a source who is useful now and one you should cultivate for future stories, and how to document your source interactions in a way that protects both you and them if your notes are ever subpoenaed.","tags":["investigative","sources","strategy","networking"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-17","title":"Right of Reply Letter Drafting","prompt":"[Describe the subject of your investigation, the specific allegations you plan to publish, and the deadline you are working toward]\n\nYou are an investigative reporter at a major regional publication. Based on the investigation described above, draft a formal right-of-reply communication that: identifies each specific allegation the subject will need to respond to, uses language that is legally precise without being inflammatory, does not reveal the identities of confidential sources, gives a specific and reasonable deadline for response, invites both a written statement and an on-the-record interview, and protects the publication by creating a documented record that the subject was given fair opportunity to comment. Then explain how to handle the five most likely responses: denial, legal threat, partial admission, no response, and a request for more time.","tags":["investigative","legal","ethics","pre-publication"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-18","title":"Prosecution of a Story: Evidence Standards","prompt":"[Describe your investigation: what you are alleging, what evidence you currently have, and what sourcing you are relying on]\n\nYou are an investigative editor who has spent 25 years evaluating whether investigations are ready to publish. Assess whether the investigation described above meets publishable evidentiary standards. Address: what the current evidence proves versus what it only suggests, the critical evidentiary gaps that remain, what would need to be true for publication to be legally and editorially defensible, the specific additional reporting required before the story can run, how to frame each allegation accurately given the current evidence, and the one thing I must do in the next 48 hours to move this story toward publication.","tags":["investigative","editing","ethics","evidentiary"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-19","title":"Shell Company Unmasking","prompt":"You are an investigative journalist who specializes in financial crimes and opaque corporate structures. I have identified a shell company — a Delaware LLC with a registered agent address — that owns a piece of real estate at the center of my investigation into a housing fraud scheme. The LLC's public filings reveal nothing about its actual owners. Walk me through the complete methodology for unmasking beneficial ownership: which state and local databases to search first, how to use FinCEN beneficial ownership records now accessible after the Corporate Transparency Act, how to cross-reference property records, UCC filings, and court documents to find names, how to use court records from civil and bankruptcy cases where LLCs must disclose principals, how to find the human beings behind a chain of nested LLCs across multiple states, when and how to use paid services like LexisNexis, TLO, or PACER, and how to document your methodology so your findings are publishable and legally defensible.","tags":["investigative","corporate","financial","research"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-20","title":"Accountability Story vs. Hit Piece Test","prompt":"You are an experienced investigative editor known for both aggressive reporting and rigorous fairness. One of the hardest distinctions in journalism is the line between a legitimate accountability story and a one-sided attack. Help me define and apply that test. Explain: the specific editorial criteria that distinguish accountability journalism from a hit piece, the role of proportionality — whether the severity of the alleged wrongdoing matches the invasiveness of the reporting methods and the prominence of the coverage, how to evaluate whether a story has treated the subject's denials and explanations with appropriate seriousness, the obligation to report exculpatory information with the same energy used to report incriminating information, how power dynamics affect these judgments (covering a sitting senator versus a low-level public employee), and how to present this framework to a reporter who is emotionally invested in a story and may have lost perspective. Give three concrete examples of editorial decisions that transform an accountability story into a hit piece.","tags":["investigative","ethics","editing","fairness"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-21","title":"Profile Writing Masterclass","prompt":"You are a magazine profile writer whose work has appeared in The Atlantic, New York Magazine, and Rolling Stone. I am writing a 4,000-word profile of a 62-year-old retired longshoreman in Baltimore who spent 30 years in the union and has become, in retirement, one of the city's most effective affordable housing advocates — operating almost entirely outside formal institutions, working phone to phone, deal to deal. I have done four hours of interviews, visited his neighborhood, and spoken to three people who know him. Help me build this profile from the ground up. Provide: the structural architecture that would work best for this subject (chronological, theme-based, or braided), how to open the piece in a scene that immediately establishes character, how to handle the tension between the subject's self-presentation and what others say about him, how to write about race and class in this story without reducing the subject to a symbol, the most important thing a profile must do that a news story does not, and a set of five follow-up questions I should ask in a final interview to fill the gaps that always appear in early drafts.","tags":["feature-writing","profiles","longform","craft"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:40:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"jou-22","title":"Scene Construction Workshop","prompt":"You are a narrative journalism instructor. The ability to write a vivid, specific, reportorial scene is one of the hardest skills in feature writing and one of the most important. Teach me how to construct a scene from reported material. Cover: the difference between a scene and a summary and when to use each, the minimum information a reporter must gather on-scene to write it compellingly (specific sensory details, quotes, physical description, action), how to handle scenes you reconstructed from interviews rather than witnessed yourself — what language you must use and what you must not claim, how to use physical detail to do character work without resorting to adjective-laden description, the pacing tools available within a scene (sentence rhythm, white space, dialogue) and how they affect the reader's experience, and how to end a scene so it earns its place in the larger story. Then write a 200-word model scene set in a hospital waiting room using only these facts: a mother has been waiting four hours for news about her 16-year-old son who was injured in a car accident; the waiting room has fluorescent lighting, a television playing a game show at low volume, and a paper cup of cold coffee.","tags":["feature-writing","narrative","craft","scenes"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-23","title":"Long-Form Story Architecture","prompt":"[Paste your draft article or notes]\n\nYou are a longform editor at a national magazine who specializes in structural reconstruction. Review the material above and help me build the architecture for a 5,000-word feature. Provide: the single strongest opening scene or moment in the material and why it should lead the piece, a recommended overall structure with section breaks and the function each section must perform, an identification of the story's central tension and whether the current material adequately develops and resolves it, the narrative thread (a character, a question, or a through-line image) that should hold the story together from beginning to end, the three places where the story currently sags and what additional reporting or writing would fix each one, and a closing scene or image that would give the story an ending that feels earned rather than merely concluded.","tags":["feature-writing","longform","structure","editing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-24","title":"The Unkillable Story Idea Generator","prompt":"You are a features editor at a large regional magazine who is famous for developing story ideas that are surprising, specific, and deeply reported. I need to break out of predictable feature story patterns. Teach me the methodology for generating genuinely original story ideas by: identifying the overlooked stories hiding inside familiar topics (the counter-intuitive angle, the ignored stakeholder, the question no one thought to ask), using public records and data releases as story idea generators rather than just reporting tools, finding stories in the gap between official narrative and lived experience, how to develop a story idea from a single overheard sentence or a strange detail in a document, the role of local knowledge and beat expertise in seeing what outsiders miss, and the test for whether an idea is truly a story versus an interesting topic. Generate five unexpected feature story ideas about a single theme — the changing nature of work in small American cities — using these techniques.","tags":["feature-writing","ideas","enterprise","creativity"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-25","title":"Writing About Trauma with Craft and Care","prompt":"You are a journalist and trauma-informed reporting specialist who has covered mass shootings, natural disasters, and community violence for 15 years. I am writing a feature about a neighborhood that experienced a cluster of gun violence deaths over a single summer, and I want to tell these stories with both journalistic rigor and genuine human care. Guide me through: how to approach interviews with people in active grief without retraumatizing them, the specific ethical obligations around photographing and identifying minors affected by violence, how to write about death in a way that honors the fullness of a person's life rather than reducing them to their circumstances, when to use and when to withhold graphic details, how to manage your own vicarious trauma as a reporter covering these stories over time, the practical steps for making resources (mental health, legal, community) available to sources and subjects, and the editorial conversations you must have with your editor before and after reporting to ensure the story serves the community rather than simply extracting from it.","tags":["feature-writing","trauma","ethics","community"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-26","title":"The Explanatory Feature Framework","prompt":"You are a senior writer at a publication known for making complex topics accessible to general audiences. I need to write a 3,000-word explanatory feature about a technically complex subject: how the Federal Reserve's interest rate decisions travel through the economy and affect the monthly mortgage payments of ordinary homeowners in a mid-size American city. This story has to be grounded in real people, rooted in real data, and free of financial jargon. Provide: a structural blueprint for an explanatory piece that moves from concrete (a specific family's situation) to abstract (the mechanism) and back to concrete (what changes), how to find and use a single central character whose experience embodies the larger phenomenon, a glossary of the five terms I must define for a general reader and the most conversational way to define each, how to use data without overwhelming the narrative, and the five most common mistakes writers make when trying to explain economics to a general audience.","tags":["feature-writing","explanatory","economics","craft"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-27","title":"Feature Ending Clinic","prompt":"You are a features editor who specializes in story endings — the closing paragraphs that make a piece linger in the reader's mind. Endings in feature journalism are notoriously difficult. Many stories simply stop; few truly end. Teach me the craft of the feature ending. Explain: the three structural options for ending a narrative feature (the full circle, the forward look, the resonant final image) and when each is appropriate, why the ending must be earned by everything that came before it and not imported from outside the story, how to find the best ending in material you've already gathered rather than writing your way to a manufactured conclusion, the difference between an ending that summarizes and one that resonates, how to write an ending that is emotionally honest without being manipulative, and the role of the final sentence's rhythm and length in creating closure. Then write two alternative endings for this scenario: a profile of a 70-year-old diner owner who is closing his restaurant after 44 years because his block has been sold to a developer. He has no regrets. His last customer was his first customer's daughter.","tags":["feature-writing","craft","endings","narrative"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-28","title":"Service Journalism Architecture","prompt":"You are a lifestyle and service journalism editor. Service journalism — the kind that tells readers how to navigate a problem or improve their lives — is one of the most widely read forms of journalism and one of the most under-theorized. Help me produce outstanding service journalism. Explain: the difference between service journalism and public relations or sponsored content, how to anchor service journalism in reporting rather than opinion or anecdote, how to structure a service piece so it is genuinely useful rather than merely interesting, the role of experts versus regular people's experience in a service story, how to make service journalism accessible to readers across different income levels and circumstances without oversimplifying, and how service journalism can have an advocacy dimension without losing journalistic credibility. Then write a 400-word service journalism lede and structure for this scenario: helping first-generation college students in a low-income rural area understand how to evaluate and compare financial aid packages.","tags":["feature-writing","service-journalism","craft","structure"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-29","title":"High-Stakes Interview Preparation","prompt":"[Describe the subject you are about to interview, what you are investigating, the evidence you already have, and any constraints the subject or their team has placed on the interview]\n\nYou are a veteran interviewer who has conducted hundreds of interviews with hostile, evasive, and powerful subjects. Based on the interview situation described above, help me prepare fully. Provide: a strategic framework for the first five minutes that establishes my right to ask about any topic, a sequence of five to seven questions that build from general to specific and are designed to make evasion progressively harder, the exact language for the pivotal accountability question, how to handle the three most likely deflection tactics given what this subject is likely to claim, how to use silence as a tool, the one question I should ask last, and how to handle the interview if the subject or their counsel attempts to end it before I have asked the key questions.","tags":["interviews","investigative","strategy","technique"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:20:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"jou-30","title":"Reluctant Source Conversion","prompt":"You are a reporter who specializes in building trust with difficult sources. I am trying to develop a source — a mid-level employee at a state regulatory agency — who has given me vague signals that something is wrong with how the agency processes complaints against nursing homes, but who pulls back every time I try to move the conversation toward specifics. They are afraid of retaliation. Walk me through the complete strategy for this situation: how to distinguish between a source who genuinely knows something and one who is overstating their access, the language and framing that reduces a source's fear without making promises you cannot keep, how to use off-the-record conversations to build trust before asking for anything on the record, the specific legal protections that might apply to this source and how to explain them accurately, how to give a reluctant source a manageable first step that does not feel like full commitment, and the ethical limits of persuasion — what you must not do even if it would work.","tags":["interviews","sources","trust","investigative"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-31","title":"Grief Interview Protocol","prompt":"You are a journalist who has spent a decade covering disasters, mass violence, and community trauma and who teaches trauma-informed interviewing. I need to interview the parents of a 19-year-old student killed in a mass shooting at their college three weeks ago. I want to do this interview because the parents have asked to speak publicly and believe their son's story deserves to be told. Give me a comprehensive guide for conducting this interview with both journalistic purpose and human care: how to begin the conversation in a way that centers their autonomy and intentions, the specific questions that are most likely to yield specific, memorable detail about who their son was as a person, how to navigate the moment when a subject breaks down and whether to continue or pause, the questions I should never ask about a violent death regardless of context, how to handle new and unexpected disclosures about the circumstances of the death, and what I owe this family after the story is published.","tags":["interviews","trauma","ethics","human-interest"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-32","title":"Expert Source Interview Technique","prompt":"You are a science and policy journalist who regularly interviews academics, researchers, and technical experts. Interviewing experts is a distinct skill from general interviewing. Help me master it. Explain: how to prepare for an interview with an expert whose work you do not fully understand, the opening questions that signal competence and get experts to engage seriously, how to ask the question 'explain this to me like I'm a smart reader who is not an expert' without being condescending, how to identify when an expert is speaking outside their actual expertise, how to push back on an expert claim without an adversarial tone, how to handle conflicting expert opinion in a single story, the difference between an expert who is genuinely the best person to interview and one who is available and quotable, and how to fact-check expert claims that are not easily verified through public documents. Give an example interview exchange for a reporter interviewing an epidemiologist about interpreting a new study on air quality and childhood asthma.","tags":["interviews","science","experts","technique"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-33","title":"The Accountability Interview Framework","prompt":"You are an investigative reporter and interview specialist. The accountability interview — where a subject is confronted with evidence of wrongdoing — is one of the most technically demanding situations in journalism. It is the moment where preparation, ethics, and skill converge. Teach me the complete framework: how to decide when to do the interview (timing relative to publication, what evidence to have in hand first), the strategic structure of the interview (starting broad before getting specific), how to present a document or piece of evidence during an interview so the subject cannot easily dismiss it, the language to use when the subject says 'I don't recall' or 'I'd have to check my records', how to remain professionally calm and procedurally fair when a subject becomes hostile or threatens legal action, the difference between persistence and badgering, and how to document the interview in a way that protects the story if the subject later claims they were misrepresented.","tags":["interviews","investigative","accountability","technique"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-34","title":"Interview Question Design Lab","prompt":"[Describe the person you are about to interview — their role, what you already know about them, and what your story needs from this interview]\n\nYou are a reporting coach who trains journalists in question design. Based on the interview subject and story needs described above, build me a complete interview guide. Provide: 10 to 14 questions ordered strategically from rapport-building to specific and difficult, with each question labeled by type (open, chronological, analytical, hypothetical, or invitational), a note on what each question is designed to yield, the two or three questions most likely to produce the story's best quotes or most important revelations, three follow-up questions to have ready for the answers you most expect, and the one question to ask last. Also flag any topic I should approach carefully or avoid early in the conversation and explain why.","tags":["interviews","craft","questions","technique"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-35","title":"Anonymous Source Handling Ethics","prompt":"You are an ethics editor at a national news organization. Anonymous sourcing is one of the most contentious and misunderstood practices in journalism — used with discipline by the best outlets and abused by the worst. I need a comprehensive, practical guide to anonymous source ethics. Address: the conditions under which anonymity is genuinely warranted versus when it is a shortcut that avoids harder reporting, the specific language rules for describing anonymous sources (what 'a person familiar with the matter' implies versus 'a senior official who spoke on condition of anonymity because they were not authorized to discuss the matter'), the newsroom policies around requiring multiple independent sources for anonymous information, the editor's role in verifying a reporter's anonymous sourcing, the legal and ethical obligations if a source's identity is later revealed through other means, and the reader's right to evaluate sourcing — how much should a publication tell readers about why anonymity was granted. Give three real-world scenarios and walk through the ethics decision for each.","tags":["ethics","sources","anonymous","standards"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-36","title":"Source Development Long Game","prompt":"You are a veteran beat reporter who has covered state government for 18 years and has cultivated sources across multiple administrations, agencies, and political parties. Source development is a long-term strategic practice, not a transactional one. Teach me how to build a source network that sustains a beat over years. Cover: how to think about sources as relationships rather than transactions, the role of being genuinely curious about a source's work in building trust, how to maintain contact with sources between stories without making it feel purely utilitarian, the ethics of reciprocity — what you can and cannot appropriately give a source in exchange for access, how to handle a source who crosses a line and starts trying to use you for their own agenda, how to keep notes on sources that protect their confidentiality if your materials are ever subpoenaed, and the ethical obligations that arise when a source you have cultivated for years confides something that is newsworthy but that they did not intend as a tip.","tags":["sources","beat","strategy","ethics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T12:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-37","title":"Pre-Publication Fact-Check System","prompt":"You are the standards editor at a major regional daily newspaper. You are designing a pre-publication fact-checking system that will be used by all staff reporters. The system must be rigorous enough to catch errors but fast enough to work on daily deadlines. Design the complete system: the fact-check log format that reporters must complete before a story goes to an editor (include all required columns), the hierarchy of evidence — what counts as primary documentation versus acceptable secondary sourcing, the escalation protocol for facts that cannot be independently verified before deadline, the specific categories of claims that require a second independent source regardless of deadline pressure (statistics, criminal allegations, medical claims, financial figures), how to handle quotes — when you must go back to the source to verify accuracy, and the post-publication correction policy including how corrections should be written, where they should appear, and how quickly they must be issued. Include a one-page fact-check checklist a reporter can print and complete for every story.","tags":["fact-checking","standards","editing","accuracy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-38","title":"Statistical Claims Verification","prompt":"[Paste the press release, political statement, or article excerpt containing the statistical claims you need to verify]\n\nYou are a data journalist and fact-checker who specializes in statistical literacy. Review the statistical claims in the content above. For each claim: identify the precise statistic being asserted, identify the original data source it should come from and how to locate it, flag any of the five common misrepresentation patterns present (cherry-picked time frames, misleading denominators, confusing relative and absolute risk, omitting base rates, or comparing non-comparable groups), explain what the number actually shows when placed in proper context, and suggest the accurate, reader-friendly language that should appear in any news story citing this claim. Conclude with an overall assessment of whether the statistical framing is honest and what a journalist must do before publishing any of these claims.","tags":["fact-checking","data","statistics","accuracy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-39","title":"Quote Accuracy Protocol","prompt":"You are a journalism ethics specialist. Quotation accuracy is a foundational obligation in journalism and one of the most frequently violated. I need a complete guide to quote handling. Address: the difference between a verbatim quote and a cleaned-up quote — when it is acceptable to remove verbal tics like 'um' and 'you know' and when it is not, what 'off the record', 'on background', and 'on the record' actually mean and how to establish ground rules before an interview begins rather than afterward, how to handle a source who calls after an interview to change or retract a quote, the ethics of composite quotes — representing the sense of what someone said across multiple conversations, the legal standard for quote alteration and when cleaning a quote crosses into defamation, how to handle quotes in languages other than English and the translator's role in accuracy, and the process for confirming quotes from a recording versus notes versus memory. Give three examples of quote edits — one that is acceptable, one that is borderline, and one that is clearly impermissible.","tags":["fact-checking","quotes","ethics","accuracy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-40","title":"Misinformation Verification Toolkit","prompt":"You are a digital verification specialist who trains journalists in open-source intelligence and fact-checking. In an era of synthetic media and viral misinformation, basic verification skills are now essential for all reporters. Build me a practical toolkit. Cover: the step-by-step process for reverse-image searching a photograph to determine its origin and whether it is being used in a false context, how to geolocate an image using visual landmarks, shadow direction, and satellite imagery, the tools and methods for detecting AI-generated images and videos at a basic level, how to verify the identity and location of a social media account making a claim, the metadata available in digital photographs and how to access and read it, how to evaluate whether a video has been edited or taken out of context, and the primary databases (Snopes, PolitiFact, FactCheck.org, Bellingcat) and how to use each efficiently. Provide a worked example: you receive a social media video claiming to show police brutality at a protest in your city from last night. Walk through every verification step.","tags":["fact-checking","digital","verification","tools"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:15:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"jou-41","title":"Political Speech Fact-Check","prompt":"[Paste your draft article or notes]\n\nYou are a political fact-checker with expertise in government budgets, legislative process, and policy claims. Review the political speech or statement pasted above. For each factual claim you identify: state the claim precisely as made, rate its verifiability (verifiable, partially verifiable, or opinion/subjective), identify the primary source that would confirm or refute it, note any context that changes the meaning of the claim even if it is technically accurate, flag any claims that are misleading through omission, and suggest a precise correction or clarification for any claims that do not withstand scrutiny. Conclude with an overall assessment of the statement's factual accuracy and a one-paragraph summary suitable for publication in a fact-check article.","tags":["fact-checking","politics","speech","accountability"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-42","title":"Science Story Fact-Check Framework","prompt":"You are a science journalist and fact-checker. Science reporting is one of the most fact-checking-intensive areas of journalism because the gap between what a study shows and what a headline claims can be enormous and the consequences of misinformation — particularly in health and medicine — can be serious. Give me a complete framework for fact-checking science stories. Cover: how to read a scientific abstract and identify the gap between findings and claims, the hierarchy of evidence (systematic review and meta-analysis versus single study versus expert opinion) and how to communicate it to readers, how to identify when a study's sample size or methodology makes its conclusions unreliable, the red flags that indicate a study may have been conducted or funded with a conflict of interest, the ethics of embargo-breaking and the risks of rushing to publish science news, how to find independent experts who can evaluate a study and how to avoid over-reliance on the same pool of media-friendly scientists, and how to handle uncertainty — when the science is genuinely unsettled and how to write about that honestly.","tags":["fact-checking","science","health","accuracy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-43","title":"Correction Culture and Standards","prompt":"You are the standards and practices editor at a digital news organization. Corrections policy is a measure of a publication's integrity. I need to build a corrections culture and system from the ground up for a newsroom of 25 journalists. Design: the internal process for identifying, escalating, and approving corrections, the correction language standards (how to write a correction that is clear, complete, and does not bury the error), the policy on what constitutes a correction versus an update versus an editor's note, how to handle corrections to stories that have been widely shared on social media where the correction will reach only a fraction of the original audience, the ethics of altering stories without noting changes, the timeframe standards (how quickly must a correction be issued once an error is confirmed), the process for handling corrections demanded by subjects of stories who may have self-interested reasons for requesting them, and how to create a newsroom environment where reporters flag their own errors rather than hoping no one notices.","tags":["corrections","standards","ethics","editing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-44","title":"Data Story Pitch Development","prompt":"You are a data journalism editor who has developed award-winning data investigations. Data without narrative is a spreadsheet; narrative without data is an anecdote. I want to develop a fully realized data journalism story. Here is my starting point: I have access to five years of 911 call response time data for a city of 600,000 people, broken down by neighborhood, time of day, and call type. Walk me through the complete story development process: how to explore this dataset for its most significant and surprising patterns, the statistical tests or comparisons most likely to yield publishable findings, how to identify whether any patterns are explained by factors I have not yet accounted for, the human stories I need to find to give the data meaning, the visualizations that would best communicate the key findings to a general audience, how to get official response and expert analysis to contextualize the data, and the ethical obligations around publishing neighborhood-level data that could reinforce or challenge existing narratives about specific communities.","tags":["data-journalism","investigation","analysis","visualization"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:35:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"jou-45","title":"Spreadsheet Journalism Basics","prompt":"You are a data journalism trainer. Many journalists are intimidated by data, but the majority of data journalism requires only spreadsheet skills that can be learned in a few days. Teach me the core spreadsheet operations that every journalist should know. Cover: sorting and filtering to find outliers and extremes, the COUNTIF and SUMIF formulas and how they answer basic journalistic questions, pivot tables and their use in summarizing large datasets, how to calculate percentage change and per-capita rates (the two most common data calculations in journalism), how to use VLOOKUP or INDEX/MATCH to combine data from two different sources, the most common errors journalists make when working with government data (formatting issues, merged cells, suppressed values), how to document your data analysis so it can be reproduced and verified by an editor, and the key questions to ask about any dataset before you trust it: who collected it, how, for what purpose, and what it might be missing.","tags":["data-journalism","spreadsheets","skills","training"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-46","title":"Public Records Data Request Strategy","prompt":"You are a data journalist who has spent 15 years acquiring and analyzing government databases. Getting the right data in the right format is as important as the analysis itself. I need a complete strategy for requesting government data. Cover: how to research what data a government agency collects before making a request, the critical importance of requesting data in a structured, machine-readable format (CSV, Excel) rather than PDF — and the exact language to use in your request, how to negotiate when an agency claims the data does not exist in the format you want, the argument for why public records law typically requires agencies to provide data in the format they maintain it, how to handle a partial production — when you receive some but not all of the data you requested, how to document your data acquisition process so it is traceable and legally defensible, and the specific agencies and databases at the federal and state level that data journalists return to most frequently, and why.","tags":["data-journalism","foia","records","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-47","title":"Data Visualization Editorial Standards","prompt":"You are a data visualization editor at a national publication. Data visualizations are powerful tools for communicating information — and powerful tools for misleading readers. I need editorial standards for data visualization in my newsroom. Address: the most common ways charts mislead (truncated y-axes, cherry-picked comparisons, dual axes with different scales, area charts that distort proportional differences), how to choose the right chart type for the type of comparison being made (change over time, part-to-whole, distribution, correlation, comparison), the accessibility requirements for data visualizations (color contrast, alt text, mobile responsiveness), how to present uncertainty and error bars in a way general readers can understand, the labeling and sourcing standards every chart must meet, when to use a table instead of a chart, and the ethics of making a visualization maximally impactful versus maximally accurate when there is tension between the two. Provide a before-and-after example of a misleading chart redesigned to be honest.","tags":["data-journalism","visualization","design","standards"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-48","title":"Census and Demographic Data Journalism","prompt":"You are a data journalist who specializes in census data and demographic analysis. Census data is one of the most powerful and most misused datasets in American journalism. Give me a comprehensive guide to using it well. Cover: the difference between the decennial census and the American Community Survey and what each is good for, how to interpret and correctly cite ACS margins of error, the specific comparisons that are valid to make with census data and those that are not, how to find and use census data at the tract and block group level for hyper-local stories, the tools available for accessing and analyzing census data (data.census.gov, IPUMS, Social Explorer), common errors journalists make when writing about race and ethnicity using census categories, how to find and report changes over time while accounting for methodological changes between surveys, and three story ideas that can be developed using only public census data for a mid-size American city.","tags":["data-journalism","census","demographics","tools"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T13:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-49","title":"Database Journalism Investigation","prompt":"[Paste your draft article or notes]\n\nYou are a data journalism editor. The reporter has gathered the data described or shared above. Help develop this into a rigorous data-driven investigation. Address: what the data can prove versus what it can only suggest, what additional data sources would strengthen or contextualize the findings, which findings are statistically significant and which may be noise or coincidence, how to structure the story so the data findings drive the narrative without overwhelming readers with numbers, the visualizations that would most clearly communicate the key findings, what independent expert analysis is needed before publication, and the specific caveats, limitations, and methodology notes that must be disclosed in the published story or an accompanying methodology box. Draft a three-paragraph methodology note explaining how the analysis was conducted.","tags":["data-journalism","investigation","analysis","editing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-50","title":"Election Data Reporting Guide","prompt":"You are an elections data journalist who has covered six election cycles for a major news organization. Election night and its aftermath is one of the highest-stakes periods for data journalism. Give me a comprehensive guide to election data reporting. Cover: the official sources for vote totals at each level of government and how to access them in real time on election night, the most common errors in early vote reporting and how to avoid them (premature calls, misattributed precinct results), how to report on vote margins and uncertainty without misleading readers about a race's outcome, the specific data comparisons that are legitimate in analyzing election results (comparing turnout across precincts, comparing candidate performance to previous cycles) and those that invite misleading conclusions, how to report on claims of election irregularity with appropriate skepticism and rigor, and how to build and maintain election result databases that are useful for years of follow-up coverage.","tags":["data-journalism","elections","politics","accuracy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-51","title":"Crime Data Reporting Ethics","prompt":"You are a criminal justice reporter and data journalism specialist. Crime data is among the most consequential and most misused data in local journalism. Give me a complete guide to responsible crime data reporting. Cover: the difference between crimes reported to police, crimes recorded in official statistics, crimes that result in arrest, and actual crime — and why these diverge significantly, how under-reporting varies by crime type, community, and the relationship between communities and police, the specific limitations of UCR (Uniform Crime Reports) data and how NIBRS (National Incident-Based Reporting System) differs, how to avoid the common error of using year-over-year changes in small area crime data to make trend claims that are statistically meaningless, the ethics of publishing crime data at the block or address level and the real-world harms this can cause, and how to report on crime without reinforcing racial or geographic stigmas while still being accurate about geographic patterns.","tags":["data-journalism","crime","ethics","criminal-justice"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-52","title":"Headline Writing Lab","prompt":"[Paste the lede and first two paragraphs of your story, or briefly describe the story and its most newsworthy finding]\n\nYou are a digital headline editor who has analyzed click-through rates, reader engagement, and journalistic quality across thousands of headlines. Based on the story above, write five headlines: (1) a hard news headline for print or homepage use, (2) an SEO-optimized digital headline, (3) a social media headline for Twitter/X, (4) a newsletter subject line, and (5) the version you would choose as your top recommendation and why. For each, explain the specific craft choice being made. Then identify any version that risks being misleading or clickbait and why, and offer a corrected alternative. Finally, diagnose the single biggest weakness in the story's content that is making headline writing harder than it should be.","tags":["headlines","editing","digital","craft"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:15:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"jou-53","title":"Editorial Strategy for a Local News Outlet","prompt":"You are a news director at a regional digital-first news outlet serving a county of 280,000 people. You have a team of six full-time reporters, a budget that has been cut 20 percent this year, and a mandate to grow both audience and sustainability. Help me build a one-year editorial strategy. Address: the beats that produce the most reader engagement and public impact per reporting hour invested, how to identify the five to eight stories per year that your outlet should own completely — investigations and enterprise pieces that justify your existence, how to use community input (reader surveys, community forums) to identify coverage gaps without letting audience demand dictate all editorial decisions, the specific types of content that drive newsletter subscriptions versus one-time visits versus sustained reader loyalty, how to partner with journalism schools, freelancers, and community organizations without compromising editorial independence, and how to measure editorial success in ways that go beyond pageviews.","tags":["editorial-strategy","management","local-news","sustainability"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-54","title":"Story Pitch Evaluation","prompt":"[Paste the story pitch you want evaluated]\n\nYou are a senior editor who receives dozens of story pitches each week. Evaluate the pitch above rigorously and fairly. Your assessment must address: whether the pitch identifies a specific news revelation or just a topic area, whether the 'so what' is clearly articulated for the specific audience, whether the reporter appears to have the story already or is proposing to go look for it (and which is more concerning given the pitch), what the story would actually contain if reported fully — the specific scenes, sources, documents, and data, the three most important questions the pitch leaves unanswered, the most promising angle that the reporter may have missed, and whether you would assign this story, kill it, send it back for development, or suggest a different approach. Be direct — the goal is to teach the reporter, not to protect their feelings.","tags":["editorial-strategy","pitches","editing","craft"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-55","title":"Newsletter Journalism Strategy","prompt":"You are a newsletter strategy consultant who has helped launch and scale more than a dozen journalism newsletters. Newsletters have become one of the most important formats in journalism, offering direct reader relationships, subscription revenue, and editorial independence. Help me build a strategy for a journalism newsletter. Cover: the editorial model that works best for newsletters (curation, original reporting, analysis, or some combination), how to establish a distinct voice and point of view that readers will pay for, the cadence and format decisions that affect open rates and reader retention, how to write newsletter prose that feels personal and direct without being performative, the metrics that actually matter for a journalism newsletter (open rate benchmarks, churn, revenue per subscriber versus total subscribers), how to grow a newsletter audience without compromising editorial integrity, and the business models available (paid subscription, advertising, grants, membership) and their trade-offs. Design a sample structure for one edition of a weekly newsletter covering state government for an educated general audience.","tags":["newsletter","editorial-strategy","digital","business"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-56","title":"Audience Development Without Clickbait","prompt":"You are an editorial director who believes that journalistic quality and audience development are not in tension — that the best journalism, well distributed, builds sustainable audiences. I am the editor of a civic journalism nonprofit covering a mid-size city. I have excellent reporters but small audiences. Help me grow the audience without compromising our standards. Address: the specific distribution strategies that work for civic journalism (social media, email, community partnerships, events), how to make serious journalism more discoverable without sensationalizing headlines or packaging, the role of community engagement — town halls, reader advisory boards, listening sessions — in both improving coverage and building audience loyalty, how to use SEO effectively for journalism without letting search optimization distort editorial decisions, the newsletters, podcasts, and social formats that are most effective for reaching audiences who do not yet know they need local news, and how to measure whether audience growth is translating into civic impact.","tags":["audience","editorial-strategy","digital","community"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-57","title":"Mobile-First Story Packaging","prompt":"You are a digital product editor at a major news organization where more than 70 percent of readers access stories on mobile devices. Mobile-first isn't just about screen size — it's about reading behavior, attention span, and the contexts in which people encounter journalism. Help me redesign how my newsroom packages stories for mobile readers. Cover: the optimal story length and structure for mobile reading of different story types (breaking news, investigative, feature, data), how subheads, pull quotes, and bullet points function differently in mobile contexts, the specific ways that long paragraphs, complex sentences, and unbroken walls of text fail mobile readers, how to integrate photos, videos, and data visualizations in ways that enhance rather than interrupt mobile reading, the difference between designing for a news app, a mobile browser, and a social media card, and how to help print-trained reporters understand that good mobile writing requires genuine structural adaptation, not just shorter paragraphs.","tags":["digital","mobile","editing","packaging"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-58","title":"Investigative Series Planning","prompt":"You are an investigative editor who has planned and executed three Pulitzer-nominated series. Planning a multi-part investigative series is categorically different from planning a single story — it requires thinking about what each installment does individually and what the series accomplishes as a whole. Walk me through the complete series planning process. Cover: how to decide what belongs in a series versus what belongs in a single comprehensive story, how to structure installments so each one stands alone while serving the larger narrative, how to sequence revelations across parts to maintain reader engagement without withholding critical information dishonestly, how to plan the publication schedule including the relationship between the first story's impact and when subsequent pieces should run, the role of companion pieces (timelines, databases, explainers, photo essays) in strengthening a series, how to coordinate the legal, editorial, and communications strategy across multiple publication dates, and how to prepare for the response — denial, legal threats, congressional attention — that a strong investigative series often provokes.","tags":["investigative","editorial-strategy","series","planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-59","title":"SEO for Journalism Without Compromise","prompt":"You are a digital editor who has studied search engine optimization specifically in the context of quality journalism. SEO is often misunderstood as the enemy of good writing, but applied carefully it can make important journalism more discoverable without compromising it. Give me a practical, ethics-grounded SEO guide for journalists. Cover: how to research the words and phrases people actually use to search for a topic and integrate them naturally into headlines and story text, the role of the headline, subhead, meta description, and opening paragraph in search ranking, why long-form, deeply reported stories often perform better in search than short takes, how to use evergreen content strategies for journalism without creating content that ages poorly, the ethics of updating stories for SEO versus updating them because information has changed, the SEO impact of internal linking and how to do it without creating editorial distortions, and what journalists should categorically refuse to do in the name of SEO.","tags":["seo","digital","headlines","editorial-strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-60","title":"Conflict of Interest Navigation","prompt":"You are a journalism ethics specialist and the ombudsman for a regional newspaper. Conflict of interest is one of the most complex areas of journalistic ethics because the conflicts journalists face are rarely black-and-white. Walk me through a comprehensive conflict of interest framework. Address: the difference between actual conflicts of interest, perceived conflicts, and potential conflicts — and why all three must be managed, the specific categories of conflict that arise most frequently (personal relationships with sources, financial interests, civic involvement, family members' employment), the newsroom policies that should govern these situations (disclosure to editors, recusal, reassignment), how to handle a conflict that arises mid-story after significant reporting has been done, the distinction between opinions expressed on a reporter's personal social media versus their professional conduct, and the special conflict-of-interest obligations of editors, who have influence over entire coverage areas rather than individual stories. Walk through four concrete scenarios: a reporter whose spouse works for the company under investigation, an editor who serves on a nonprofit board that is a story subject, a reporter who is friends with a political candidate, and a journalist who owns stock in an industry they cover.","tags":["ethics","conflicts","standards","governance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T14:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-61","title":"Reporting on Minors: Ethics and Law","prompt":"You are a journalism ethics and law specialist. Reporting on and about minors is one of the most ethically and legally sensitive areas of journalism, governed by both legal requirements and professional ethical standards that often go beyond what the law requires. Give me a comprehensive guide. Cover: the legal landscape around publishing the names and images of minors in different contexts (juvenile criminal proceedings, schools, sports, crime victims), the newsroom policies most major publications maintain regarding minors and how they differ from legal requirements, the specific situations where publishing a minor's identity may be both legal and nevertheless harmful, how to make age-appropriate decisions about interviewing minors (the question of parental consent versus the minor's own autonomy in different circumstances), the handling of minors in online and social media contexts, how to cover school shootings, child abuse, and juvenile crime with appropriate care, and how to evaluate whether the public interest in identifying a minor outweighs the harm to that individual.","tags":["ethics","minors","legal","standards"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-62","title":"Covering Suicide: Responsible Reporting","prompt":"You are a journalism ethics specialist with expertise in mental health reporting and the research on media contagion effects. Responsible suicide reporting is one of the most evidence-based areas of journalism ethics — there is strong research showing that irresponsible coverage can increase suicide rates. Give me a comprehensive guide for my newsroom. Cover: the specific reporting practices that research has linked to contagion effects (detailed method description, prominent placement, romanticization) and those linked to protective effects, how the Reporting on Suicide guidelines (ReportingOnSuicide.org) should be implemented practically in a deadline environment, when suicide is genuinely newsworthy and how to report on it in those cases (a public official, a pattern of deaths in a community, a policy story), the language standards that reflect clinical accuracy and reduce stigma, how to interview survivors, family members, and witnesses with care, and how to include mental health resources in coverage without it feeling perfunctory. Address the specific challenge of covering high-profile celebrity deaths by suicide.","tags":["ethics","mental-health","standards","community"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-63","title":"Algorithmic Accountability Reporting","prompt":"You are an investigative journalist who specializes in technology accountability, with a focus on automated decision-making systems. Algorithms now make consequential decisions about who gets a mortgage, whose benefits are cut, who is flagged for deportation, and who is recommended for parole. Reporting on these systems is both urgent and technically demanding. Help me develop a reporting approach. Cover: how to identify when a government agency or corporation is using an automated system to make decisions that were previously made by humans, the public records strategies for obtaining documentation about algorithmic systems (contracts, audits, impact assessments, source code where applicable), the experts I need to understand the technical and civil liberties dimensions of algorithmic accountability, how to find the people harmed by algorithmic decisions and how to document their cases, the specific questions to ask a vendor or government agency about their system's accuracy, bias testing, and appeal processes, and how to write about technical systems accurately for a general audience without either sensationalizing or obscuring the real issues.","tags":["investigative","technology","accountability","ai"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:10:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"jou-64","title":"Diversity, Equity and Source Inclusion Audit","prompt":"You are a diversity and inclusion consultant for newsrooms with a background in editorial standards. Who we interview, quote, and feature in our journalism shapes whose voices and perspectives are centered and whose are marginalized. I want to conduct a rigorous source diversity audit for my newsroom. Design the audit. Cover: how to measure the demographic breakdown of sources quoted in stories by beat and reporter over a six-month period, the specific categories to track (gender, race/ethnicity, organizational affiliation, geography, age, disability status where relevant), how to distinguish between the diversity problem in story sourcing versus the diversity problem in story selection (which stories get covered at all), how to address implicit biases in source selection that are structural rather than individual, the practical strategies reporters can use to diversify their source pools without tokenizing or stereotyping, and how to evaluate progress in a way that improves journalism quality rather than just hitting demographic targets. Include a template for a beat-level source diversity review.","tags":["ethics","diversity","inclusion","standards"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-65","title":"Covering Extremism Without Amplifying It","prompt":"You are a journalism ethics expert who has advised newsrooms on covering political extremism, hate groups, and radicalization. Covering extremism responsibly requires navigating a genuine tension: informing the public about dangerous movements versus giving those movements a platform that aids in recruitment and normalization. Give me a principled and practical framework. Cover: how to decide when an extremist individual or group is genuinely newsworthy versus when coverage serves their interests more than the public's, the specific practices to avoid that have been shown to benefit extremist recruitment (humanizing profiles without accountability framing, repeating extremist rhetoric in headlines), how to report on extremist claims and conspiracy theories without amplifying them (the inoculation approach), how to verify the size and reach of an extremist movement without relying on the movement's own self-reporting, the safety and security considerations for reporters covering violent extremism, and how to cover communities affected by extremism — the targets, the families of radicalized individuals — without sensationalizing their experiences.","tags":["ethics","extremism","standards","safety"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-66","title":"Artificial Intelligence in the Newsroom: Ethics Policy","prompt":"You are a journalism ethics specialist tasked with drafting your news organization's policy on the use of artificial intelligence tools in reporting and production. This is a rapidly evolving area where many publications have made decisions they later regretted. Help me build a comprehensive, durable policy framework. Address: the editorial tasks for which AI assistance is acceptable (research, transcription, translation, headline suggestions) and those for which it is not (original reported content, fabricated quotes or composites, bylined analysis), the disclosure obligations to readers when AI tools have been used in the production of a story, the accuracy risks specific to large language models and how to prevent them from introducing errors into published work, how to handle AI-generated images and synthetic media in the verification and fact-checking workflow, the intellectual property considerations when using AI tools trained on copyrighted journalism, and the editorial governance process for approving new AI tools. Draft the first three paragraphs of a public-facing AI ethics statement suitable for your publication's about page.","tags":["ethics","ai","standards","policy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-67","title":"Privacy Versus Public Interest Decision Framework","prompt":"You are a journalism ethics professor who has written extensively on the right to privacy and the public interest. Journalism regularly invades privacy in service of the public interest, and the ethics of these decisions are often genuinely difficult. Help me build a decision framework for my newsroom. The framework should address: the spectrum from fully public figures (elected officials in their official capacity) to fully private individuals and how expectations of privacy change along that spectrum, the specific factors that can justify reporting on an otherwise private matter (relevance to official duties, public safety, fraud, hypocrisy), the ethical difference between information that is technically public (court records, public Facebook posts) and information that was not intended for public consumption, how to handle information about private family members of public figures who did not choose public life, the heightened privacy obligations in health, mental health, and sexual history, and the role of newsworthiness as a legal concept versus a genuine ethical justification. Walk through five specific scenarios and apply the framework to each.","tags":["ethics","privacy","standards","legal"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-68","title":"Documentary Journalism Planning","prompt":"[Describe your documentary project idea: the subject, what you already know, your current access, and what you hope to show]\n\nYou are an award-winning documentary journalist whose films have appeared at Sundance and led to policy changes. Based on the project described above, help me plan this documentary journalism work. Address: how documentary journalism differs from print investigative work in access challenges, storytelling tools, and ethical obligations as they apply to this specific project, the access strategy — how to build the institutional relationships and legal standing needed to film in this environment, the specific ethical obligations around filming the people at the center of this story given their circumstances, how to identify and develop the two or three central characters who will carry the film's emotional and narrative weight, the visual storytelling techniques that translate the core complexity into human story, the funding and production realities to plan for, and the one thing that would most likely kill this project and how to prevent it.","tags":["documentary","investigative","film","ethics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-69","title":"Photojournalism Ethics in Conflict","prompt":"You are a photojournalism ethics expert and veteran conflict photographer. The camera creates power over its subjects that carries profound ethical weight, and nowhere is this more acute than in conflict zones and communities in crisis. Give me a comprehensive ethics framework for conflict photojournalism. Cover: the decision of whether to photograph a dying or dead person and under what conditions it is ethical to publish such images, the ethics of the decisive moment — whether to photograph or intervene in a humanitarian crisis, how to obtain informed consent in chaotic, multilingual, or dangerous contexts, the specific risks to local subjects whose images appear in international media — identification, retaliation, family impact — and how to mitigate them, how to resist editorial pressure to provide the most dramatic image rather than the most representative or truthful one, the responsibility to your fixer, translator, and local colleagues who face risks that you as a foreign journalist do not, and the long-term psychological effects on photojournalists covering traumatic events.","tags":["photojournalism","ethics","conflict","visual"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-70","title":"Visual Storytelling for Text Journalists","prompt":"You are a multimedia editor who trains text journalists in visual storytelling. In modern journalism, every reporter benefits from understanding visual narrative — whether they are writing captions, briefing photographers, editing video packages, or thinking about how their story will be packaged. Give text journalists a practical visual literacy curriculum. Cover: how a photograph communicates information that text cannot and vice versa, and how to think about them as complements rather than redundancies, how to brief a photographer or videographer so they understand the story's key moments and emotional register, how to write captions that add information rather than describe what is already visible, how to evaluate whether a photograph is being used to illustrate a point versus to tell a story, the specific ethical rules for photo editing — what manipulation is acceptable and what is not, the role of photo essays and visual narratives in journalism, and how to plan the visual elements of a story during the reporting phase rather than after writing is complete.","tags":["photojournalism","visual","multimedia","craft"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-71","title":"Drone Journalism: Ethics and Law","prompt":"You are a multimedia journalist and aviation law specialist who has written the drone journalism guidelines for a major regional news organization. Drone journalism opens up visual storytelling possibilities that were previously available only to the largest outlets, but it also creates legal and ethical obligations that many newsrooms handle poorly. Build me a complete framework. Cover: the FAA regulations governing commercial drone operations, the Part 107 certification requirement, and the airspace restrictions most likely to affect news coverage, the specific situations where newsworthy events create legal or ethical pressure to fly in restricted airspace and how to handle them, the privacy laws that apply to drone footage — the difference between public spaces and private property viewed from the air, how to balance the visual power of aerial footage with the dignity and privacy of the people photographed from above, the newsgathering contexts where drone use is most appropriate (disaster coverage, environmental investigations, large-scale events) and those where it is inappropriate, and the safety protocols every newsroom drone program must maintain.","tags":["drone","legal","photojournalism","multimedia"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-72","title":"Documentary Subject Relationship Ethics","prompt":"You are a documentary ethics specialist who has studied the relationship between filmmakers and their subjects across long-term documentary projects. The extended, intimate nature of documentary work creates ethical obligations that do not arise in shorter-form journalism. Give me a comprehensive guide to managing the filmmaker-subject relationship responsibly. Cover: how to give subjects a realistic understanding of what participation means — including the filmmaker's editorial control, the risk of unflattering portrayal, and the public nature of the final product, the ethics of the evolving relationship, as subjects who become friends or confidants may expect editorial accommodations you cannot in good conscience provide, how to handle a subject who wants to withdraw after the film is substantially complete, the specific obligations around vulnerable subjects — children, people in addiction or mental health crisis, people in poverty — whose situation may affect their ability to give fully informed consent, how to present subjects with complexity and humanity without producing advocacy that undermines journalistic credibility, and the filmmaker's obligations after the film is released, particularly if the film has negative consequences for its subjects.","tags":["documentary","ethics","subjects","relationships"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T15:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-73","title":"Photo Caption Writing Standards","prompt":"You are a photo editor who has written and edited tens of thousands of captions. The caption is among the most-read text in any publication — research consistently shows readers go to images and captions before body text — yet caption writing is often treated as an afterthought. Give me a complete caption writing guide. Cover: the structure of a complete caption (identification of all pictured individuals from left to right, context, date, location, credit), how much information a caption should provide and how it should complement rather than duplicate the story, the ethical obligation to identify everyone in a photograph and what to do when identification is incomplete, how to caption photographs of people in distress without being exploitative or sensationalizing, the correct handling of handout photographs from organizations — how to label them and why the sourcing matters editorially, how to caption images from historical archives or file photos used in current stories, and the five most common caption errors (wrong identification, tense problems, caption-story contradiction, stating the obvious, and missing credit) with corrected examples for each.","tags":["photojournalism","captions","craft","standards"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-74","title":"Immersive and VR Journalism Ethics","prompt":"You are a digital innovation editor and journalism ethics researcher who has studied immersive journalism — virtual reality, augmented reality, and 360-degree video — as it has emerged as a storytelling medium. Immersive journalism creates ethical questions that traditional journalism has not had to confront. Give me a principled framework. Cover: the specific ethical risks of immersive journalism's claim to place audiences 'inside' a situation or perspective — the risk of false empathy and the misrepresentation of experience, the consent obligations when subjects' environments are captured in 360 degrees rather than from a single camera angle, how to handle reconstructed or simulated scenes in VR journalism and what disclosure is required, the accessibility limitations of immersive journalism and the obligation to make stories available in non-immersive formats, the power dynamics of placing audiences inside crisis situations (refugee camps, combat zones, disaster sites) and whether this is journalism or exploitation, and the standards for editorial accuracy in immersive environments where the audience cannot separate the created from the captured.","tags":["digital","vr","ethics","multimedia"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-75","title":"Press Freedom Legal Toolkit","prompt":"You are a media lawyer and First Amendment specialist who advises journalists and news organizations. Understanding the legal framework that protects — and limits — press freedom is essential for every working journalist. Give me a comprehensive legal literacy guide covering: the First Amendment protections for newsgathering and publication, including the areas where courts have found these protections weaker than journalists assume, the legal definition of defamation and the specific elements a plaintiff must prove — and how public figure status dramatically changes the standard, the reporter's privilege — the legal protection for confidential sources — and how it varies dramatically across states and does not exist under federal law in the way many journalists believe, the key legal distinctions in newsgathering: trespass, misrepresentation, recording consent laws (one-party versus two-party states), and tortious interference, what to do if you receive a subpoena for your notes, recordings, or source information, and the specific legal protections that apply to digital communications and unpublished newsgathering materials.","tags":["press-freedom","legal","rights","standards"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:10:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"jou-76","title":"Journalist Safety Planning for Hostile Environments","prompt":"You are a journalist safety trainer who has worked with organizations including the Committee to Protect Journalists and the Dart Center for Journalism and Trauma. Journalism in hostile environments — protest coverage, conflict zones, communities where journalists are targeted — requires specific safety planning. Give me a comprehensive pre-deployment safety planning framework for a reporter covering domestic civil unrest and protests. Cover: the pre-assignment risk assessment process, the gear that reduces risk (eye protection, hearing protection, press identification), the legal rights of journalists covering public demonstrations and law enforcement responses to them, how to establish communication protocols with your editor including check-in intervals and emergency escalation, how to recognize and respond to changing threat levels in a crowd situation, the specific risks of being misidentified as a protester versus a law enforcement asset, digital security before and during coverage (phone encryption, secure communications), and psychological preparation for covering traumatic events and the newsroom's obligations to reporter mental health after difficult assignments.","tags":["safety","press-freedom","operations","training"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-77","title":"Digital Security for Journalists","prompt":"You are a digital security trainer who works specifically with journalists and news organizations. Digital threats to journalists have grown dramatically — from state-sponsored surveillance to targeted harassment campaigns to the hacking of newsroom systems. Give me a comprehensive digital security guide for working journalists. Cover: the specific threats that journalists face (device seizure, network interception, targeted surveillance, account compromise, and doxxing) and which threat model applies to which type of reporting, the baseline security practices every journalist should maintain regardless of beat (strong passwords, two-factor authentication, device encryption, secure messaging), the additional measures required for reporters working on sensitive investigations involving government sources (Tor, Signal, air-gapped computers, operational security discipline), how to protect sources' digital security and communications metadata — not just content encryption but traffic analysis, how to handle device seizure at international borders, and how to conduct a threat assessment before beginning a sensitive investigation so that your security measures are proportionate to the actual risks.","tags":["digital-security","safety","privacy","investigative"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-78","title":"Covering Press Freedom Internationally","prompt":"You are a foreign correspondent and press freedom advocate who has covered journalism in authoritarian states for 20 years. Understanding the global state of press freedom — and reporting on it — is one of the most important beat responsibilities in international journalism. Give me a comprehensive framework for covering press freedom as a story. Cover: the key organizations that track press freedom globally (Reporters Without Borders, Committee to Protect Journalists, Freedom of the Press Foundation) and how to use and critically evaluate their rankings and reports, the specific legal mechanisms authoritarian governments use to suppress journalism (strategic litigation, accreditation denial, equipment seizure, source prosecution under espionage law), how to safely interview journalists working under authoritarian constraints and protect them from the consequences of speaking, how to report on media ownership concentration and its impact on editorial independence, the distinction between de jure press freedom (constitutional protections) and de facto press freedom (whether journalists can actually report without fear), and how to cover press freedom threats in democracies including the United States.","tags":["press-freedom","international","human-rights","reporting"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-79","title":"Handling Legal Threats and Intimidation","prompt":"[Describe the legal threat or intimidation you have received — the letter, demand, or threat, and the story it relates to]\n\nYou are a media lawyer who advises news organizations on responding to legal pressure campaigns against journalism. Based on the situation described above, help me respond appropriately. Address: whether this appears to be a legitimate legal concern or a SLAPP suit (Strategic Lawsuit Against Public Participation) designed to intimidate, what anti-SLAPP laws may apply in the relevant jurisdiction and how they work, the immediate steps I should take — what to do and what not to do in the next 24 hours, how to escalate this internally and when outside legal counsel is essential, whether and how this threat should affect any editorial decisions about the story, the specific elements in the story that create the most legal exposure and whether they can be addressed without compromising the journalism, and how to document everything that happens from this point forward.","tags":["press-freedom","legal","safety","standards"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-80","title":"Online Harassment of Journalists: Response Protocol","prompt":"You are a journalist safety specialist and researcher who has studied the systematic harassment of journalists, particularly women and journalists of color, in online environments. Online harassment has become a serious occupational hazard for journalists, affecting their safety, their mental health, and their editorial decisions. Help me build a newsroom response protocol. Cover: the spectrum of online harassment journalists face, from trolling to targeted campaigns to physical threats, and how to triage responses appropriately, the documentation practices journalists should maintain from the moment harassment begins (screenshots, timestamps, platform identifiers), when and how to engage with law enforcement, the specific platforms' reporting mechanisms and their varying effectiveness, the newsroom's institutional obligations to support targeted journalists, including security assessment, legal support, and mental health resources, the social media practices that reduce vulnerability without requiring journalists to go fully dark, and the specific risks faced by journalists who cover hate groups, extremism, or political polarization and how they should adapt their digital footprint accordingly.","tags":["safety","harassment","digital","newsroom"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-81","title":"Covering Your Own Newsroom's Misconduct","prompt":"You are an ethics editor who has navigated one of the most difficult situations in journalism: reporting on misconduct within your own organization. This situation tests every principle of journalistic independence and creates genuine and complex conflicts of interest. Give me a framework for navigating it. Cover: the conditions under which a newsroom is obligated to report on itself with the same rigor it applies to external institutions, how to manage the conflict of interest when senior editors or owners are involved in the misconduct — who makes editorial decisions, who is recused, and whether outside oversight is appropriate, the specific legal and ethical protections for journalists within a newsroom who report on their colleagues or employers, how to handle the competing loyalties — to your colleagues, to your employer, and to your readers and the public — when they come into direct conflict, the role of press critics, media beat reporters at competing outlets, and journalism reviews in providing external accountability, and how to communicate with your audience about the steps you are taking to investigate and correct institutional failures.","tags":["ethics","press-freedom","standards","governance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-82","title":"Foreign Correspondent Safety and Preparation","prompt":"You are a foreign correspondent safety trainer who has worked with major international news organizations. Preparing journalists to report safely from conflict zones and high-risk environments is a professional and organizational responsibility. Build me a comprehensive pre-deployment preparation framework for a journalist being sent to a conflict zone for the first time. Cover: the mandatory training that responsible news organizations should provide before deployment (hostile environment and first aid training, digital security, psychological preparation), the research and intelligence-gathering process before arrival (contact lists, geography, political landscape, specific risks in the target area), the communication protocols and check-in procedures with the home office, the specific decisions to make before you leave (insurance, next-of-kin instructions, will and healthcare proxy documents), how to identify and work safely with a local fixer and the ethical obligations to that person, how to manage relationships with military or armed faction escorts, and the psychological and physical re-entry process upon returning from an intense or traumatic assignment.","tags":["safety","international","training","operations"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-83","title":"Science Journalism Translation Masterclass","prompt":"You are a senior science journalist whose work has appeared in Science, Nature, and The New York Times Science section. You have spent 20 years translating complex scientific research for general audiences without dumbing it down or sacrificing accuracy. Teach me your methodology. Cover: how to read a scientific paper — the structure of a paper and which sections to read first for a journalist, what to look for in the methods section that tells you whether results are trustworthy, how to evaluate sample size and statistical significance in plain terms, the specific language bridges between scientific precision and reader-friendly explanation (the use of analogy, scale, and concrete consequence), how to identify the gap between what a paper proves and what the authors or press release claim it proves, how to find expert commentators who are not the study authors and what questions to ask them, the ethics of embargo in science journalism and when breaking one is justified, and how to convey genuine scientific uncertainty without undermining readers' ability to make decisions. Write a 200-word example of a complex climate finding translated for a general newspaper audience.","tags":["science","scicomm","craft","translation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-84","title":"Court Document Translation and Reporting","prompt":"[Paste the court document, charging document, civil complaint, or court filing you need help understanding and reporting on]\n\nYou are a legal affairs reporter who has covered federal and state courts for 15 years. Review the court document above and help me report on it accurately. Provide: a plain-language summary of what the document says and what it means legally, identification of the most newsworthy claims or findings in the document and why each matters, any technical or legal terms that require explanation for a general audience and how to define them simply, any language in the document that is an allegation rather than a finding, and how to report on it accurately without convicting anyone in print, the additional documents or court records I should obtain to fully understand this proceeding, the sources I should contact for comment or context, and a suggested lede for a news story based on this document.","tags":["legal","courts","reporting","craft"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T16:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-85","title":"Health and Medical Reporting Fundamentals","prompt":"You are a medical journalist and health reporter who has covered the healthcare system, public health, and medical research for a national publication for 12 years. Health journalism that misleads readers has real-world consequences: it can drive people to ineffective treatments, generate unfounded fear, or undermine public health campaigns. Give me a comprehensive guide to responsible health journalism. Cover: how to evaluate a medical study before reporting on it — sample size, study design, peer review status, conflict of interest disclosures, how to distinguish between correlation and causation in health research and explain the difference to readers, the specific language that overstates certainty ('breakthrough', 'cure', 'causes') and the more accurate alternatives, how to cover drug approvals and medical device recalls with appropriate regulatory context, the ethics of covering individual patients and their medical information, how to report on health disparities across race, income, and geography without reinforcing harmful narratives, and how to find and use community health data to localize national health stories.","tags":["health","science","craft","accuracy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-86","title":"Political Story Accountability Audit","prompt":"[Paste the political story, campaign coverage, or policy article you want reviewed]\n\nYou are a political journalist and press critic who has argued for years that too much political coverage focuses on polling, strategy, and gamesmanship rather than what voters need to know. Review the political story above from a substance and accountability perspective. Identify: any horse-race or strategic framing that substitutes for substantive coverage of policy and record, factual claims from political actors that appear to have been passed through without verification or context, the sources cited and whether they are balanced in a way that adds information or falsely equalizing in a way that misleads, missing context about the candidates' or officials' records, positions, or conflicts of interest that would change how readers understand the story, the single most important question the story fails to answer, and specific revisions that would make this story more useful to a voter making a decision or a citizen evaluating their government.","tags":["politics","craft","accountability","standards"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-87","title":"Business and Finance Reporting for General Audiences","prompt":"You are a business journalist who has written financial coverage for both specialist and general audiences at publications including Bloomberg and a major metropolitan daily. The same story requires fundamentally different treatment for these two audiences. Help me master the translation. Cover: the financial concepts that appear most frequently in business news stories and how to define each for a general reader in one sentence (earnings, revenue, market capitalization, quarterly guidance, short selling, bond yields), how to localize a national financial story so it means something to readers in a specific community, how to use company financial filings (10-K, 10-Q, earnings calls) as reporting tools rather than just technical documents, how to find and develop business sources who will talk candidly in an environment where many potential sources have legal constraints on what they can say, the ethics of business journalism and the specific pressures that can compromise it (access journalism, advertiser pressure, the revolving door between journalism and the industries covered), and how to write about economic inequality without being either polemical or falsely neutral.","tags":["business","finance","craft","translation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-88","title":"Immigration Story Review","prompt":"[Paste your draft immigration story, scene notes, or reporting summary]\n\nYou are an immigration reporter who has covered the immigration system for a national publication for eight years and who has written about the specific harms that careless immigration journalism can cause. Review the draft or notes above. Address: any language choices that use contested or dehumanizing framing — 'illegal immigrant' versus 'undocumented immigrant' versus 'person without legal status' — and what the AP Style guidance is, any legal or procedural claims about the immigration process that need verification or clarification (asylum standards, deportation timelines, court processes), whether the people most affected by the story are present in the draft with adequate voice and specificity, any source or framing imbalances that reproduce political talking points rather than adding accurate context, how to better protect any sources with vulnerable immigration status, and the single most important addition or revision that would make this story more accurate and more dignified.","tags":["immigration","ethics","language","craft"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-89","title":"Freelance Journalism Business Survival","prompt":"You are a veteran freelance journalist who has built a 15-year career contributing to major national and international publications while maintaining financial sustainability and editorial independence. Freelance journalism requires managing a business while practicing a craft. Give me a comprehensive survival guide. Cover: how to build and maintain relationships with multiple editors at multiple publications so your income is not dependent on a single relationship, the contract terms that freelancers should understand and those they should push back on (rights grabs, kill fees, payment timelines, indemnification clauses), how to research and pitch stories that match the audience, budget, and editorial appetite of the specific publication you are targeting, how to negotiate rates from a position of knowledge — what the market pays for different types of assignments at different types of publications, the financial planning realities of freelance journalism (health insurance, taxes, income volatility, the necessity of cash reserves), how to protect your editorial independence when financial pressure is constant, and how to build a sustainable mix of well-paid work that may be less interesting and underpaid work that matters to you editorially.","tags":["freelance","career","business","craft"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-90","title":"Podcast Journalism Production Guide","prompt":"You are a podcast journalism producer whose work has won Peabody and duPont awards. Audio journalism is a distinct craft — the same story that works in print requires different choices in sound. Help me produce better journalism podcasts. Cover: the specific ways audio storytelling differs from print narrative (no white space, the primacy of the voice, the power and limitation of ambient sound), how to conduct and record interviews for audio use, including microphone technique, room acoustics, and how to use silence and breath intentionally, the editorial structure that works best for a narrative journalism podcast (cold open, act structure, the role of the host voice in threading scenes), how to write narration that sounds spoken rather than read — the sentence rhythms, vocabulary choices, and contractions that make audio narration feel natural, the ethical obligations around tape editing — what constitutes acceptable editing and what constitutes manufacturing a quote or false juxtaposition, how to use music and sound design to support rather than manipulate the journalism, and the production workflow from raw tape to final episode.","tags":["audio","podcast","craft","production"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:25:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"jou-91","title":"Newsletter Writing Voice and Craft","prompt":"[Paste your draft article or notes]\n\nYou are a newsletter writing coach who has helped dozens of journalists develop the distinctive voice that makes a newsletter feel like correspondence rather than content. Review the draft or notes above and help me develop or strengthen the newsletter voice. Address: whether the opening creates an immediate sense of the writer's personality and perspective, whether the prose rhythm is appropriate for reading on a screen and in an inbox (shorter sentences, more white space, more direct address), where the writing is formal in a way that creates distance from the reader, the specific word choices and sentence constructions that feel more like broadcast narration than conversation, whether the structure makes it easy to scan and find value quickly, how to integrate links and references without disrupting the reading flow, and the one fundamental change that would most improve how this piece reads as a newsletter rather than as a news article.","tags":["newsletter","voice","craft","editing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-92","title":"Environmental Story Editorial Review","prompt":"[Paste your draft environmental or climate story, or your reporting notes and angle]\n\nYou are an environmental journalist who has covered climate, pollution, and ecological systems for 12 years. Review the draft or notes above and provide a rigorous editorial assessment. Address: any false balance in the framing — places where the story gives equivalent weight to scientific consensus and fringe dissent, any local or community dimension that is missing or underdeveloped, whether the climate or environmental science is accurately characterized or overstated, the regulatory and enforcement context that would help readers understand what, if anything, is being done about the problem, any environmental justice dimension — who is most affected and whether their voices are present, the additional data sources or documents that would strengthen the story's evidentiary base, and the one revision that would most improve the story's accuracy, fairness, and impact.","tags":["environment","climate","craft","ethics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-93","title":"Narrative Pacing and Structure Diagnosis","prompt":"[Paste your draft narrative or longform piece, or a substantial excerpt of at least 500 words]\n\nYou are a narrative nonfiction editor whose work has been published in The New Yorker, Esquire, and Harper's Magazine. Review the draft above for pacing, time management, and structural flow. Diagnose: where the story moves too fast and a scene or moment deserves more space, where it moves too slowly and a summary could replace extended scene, any time transitions that are disorienting or unclear, whether flashbacks or flash-forwards are earning their place or creating unnecessary confusion, the places where sentence and paragraph rhythm creates the wrong energy for the moment — urgency where the story needs contemplation, or meandering where it needs pace, and the single structural or pacing change that would most dramatically improve the reader's experience of this piece. Provide specific suggested rewrites for the two or three most critical pacing problems.","tags":["narrative","craft","pacing","longform"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-94","title":"Reporting Across Language and Culture","prompt":"You are a foreign correspondent and multilingual journalist who has reported from 40 countries and worked extensively with translators, fixers, and local journalists. Reporting across language and cultural barriers is one of the most demanding aspects of international journalism and one of the most consequential for accuracy and fairness. Give me a comprehensive guide. Cover: how to work effectively with a translator — briefing them on the goals of the interview, managing the rhythm of translated conversation, and verifying that the translation captures nuance rather than just words, how to evaluate a fixer's knowledge, reliability, and potential conflicts of interest, the specific risks of cultural misinterpretation in both interviewing (different norms around directness, hierarchy, and hospitality) and in writing (the risk of imposing a foreign narrative framework on local events), how to quote non-English speakers in a way that is both accurate and fair — the ethics of editing translated quotes for clarity, the obligation to make sources sound like themselves rather than like broken English, and how to acknowledge the limits of your own cultural knowledge in your journalism and when to involve local partners in editorial decisions.","tags":["international","translation","cultural","craft"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-95","title":"Explanatory Journalism Planning","prompt":"[Describe the complex institution, policy, or civic process you need to explain to a general audience, and what specifically is hard for readers to understand about it]\n\nYou are a journalist and civics education specialist who has produced award-winning explanatory journalism about democratic institutions. Based on the topic described above, help me plan an explanatory piece that makes this genuinely accessible without oversimplifying. Provide: the two or three human stories or concrete examples that would make the abstract process real and meaningful for readers, the five to eight key concepts I must explain and the clearest, most concrete way to define each, how to demonstrate why this process or institution matters for the daily lives of ordinary readers, the expert sources most likely to make this comprehensible and engaging rather than abstract, a structural blueprint for the piece (from concrete to abstract to concrete again, or another approach that fits this topic), and a test for whether the explanatory journalism has succeeded — what a reader should be able to explain to a friend after reading this piece.","tags":["explanatory","democracy","institutions","civics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-96","title":"Mental Health Coverage Language Audit","prompt":"[Paste your draft story or article excerpt that involves mental health, addiction, or psychiatric context]\n\nYou are a mental health journalist and reporting trainer who has developed newsroom guidelines for mental health coverage. Review the draft above for accuracy, language, and ethical handling of mental health content. Flag: any language that uses stigmatizing or clinically inaccurate framing (identity-first versus person-first language, misuse of clinical terms), any implied connection between mental illness and violence that is not supported by epidemiological evidence, any claims about an individual's diagnosis or treatment that should be withheld or handled differently, whether the story's sources include people with lived experience and not just clinical or official perspectives, missing context about the mental health system that would help readers understand the story's significance, and the two or three specific word or sentence changes that would make the story more accurate, less harmful, and more useful to readers who may themselves be affected by these conditions.","tags":["mental-health","ethics","language","standards"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T17:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-97","title":"Sports Journalism Beyond the Game","prompt":"You are a sports journalist who has written for Sports Illustrated and ESPN and who is known for using sports as a lens to examine broader social, political, and economic questions. Sports journalism at its best is journalism that happens to be about sports — it applies the same rigor, the same accountability instincts, and the same commitment to truth that characterizes great journalism in any domain. Help me think beyond game coverage. Cover: how to identify the business, political, and social stories hiding inside sports institutions (stadium financing, labor relations, the economics of college athletics, the political economy of sports media), how to use athlete profiles and sports narratives to illuminate questions of race, class, gender, and power in American life, the investigative reporting tools that apply specifically to sports (salary databases, collective bargaining agreements, Title IX compliance data, NIL financial disclosures), how to maintain independence from teams and leagues that control access, and how to cover sports-related health stories — CTE, athlete mental health, performance-enhancing drugs — with appropriate scientific rigor.","tags":["sports","investigative","accountability","craft"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-98","title":"Draft Article Diagnostic and Revision","prompt":"[Paste your draft article or notes]\n\nYou are a senior editor who has revised thousands of news and feature stories. Conduct a thorough diagnostic on the draft above and produce a prioritized revision guide. Your assessment must cover: the single most important structural problem in the piece and how to fix it, any lede or opening that fails to earn its place and a suggested revision, the nut graf — whether it exists, where it is, and whether it does its job, all factual claims that require additional attribution or verification before publication, quotes that are doing no work and should be cut or replaced, sections where the pacing breaks down and the reader is likely to disengage, any language that is jargon-heavy, passive, or abstract when active and concrete would serve better, the ending and whether it resolves the story or simply stops, and the overall question of whether this story delivers on the promise of its opening. Provide your feedback as an ordered revision list from most critical to least, with specific suggested edits for the top three items.","tags":["editing","revision","craft","news-writing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:05:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"jou-99","title":"Ethics Case Analysis","prompt":"[Describe the journalism ethics dilemma you are facing — the situation, the competing obligations, and the decision you need to make]\n\nYou are a journalism ethics specialist who uses rigorous case analysis to help journalists navigate difficult decisions. Based on the situation described above, provide a complete ethics analysis. Address: the specific competing values and obligations at stake (accuracy, fairness, public interest, source protection, harm minimization, legal exposure), the questions that must be answered before any decision is made, who within the newsroom should be involved in this decision and why, the range of options available — from most aggressive to most cautious — and the ethical case for each, the option you recommend and the reasoning that supports it, and what this decision should be documented as a matter of editorial policy so it informs future decisions in similar situations. Be direct about where reasonable journalists might disagree and why the choice still must be made.","tags":["ethics","training","case-study","standards"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"jou-100","title":"The Future of Local News: Sustainability Models","prompt":"You are a journalism business analyst and local news researcher who studies models for financially sustainable local journalism. Local news is in a structural crisis — advertising revenue has collapsed, newsroom employment has fallen by more than 60 percent since 2008, and hundreds of communities have lost all local coverage. Yet innovative models are emerging. Give me a comprehensive landscape of sustainability approaches. Cover: the nonprofit local news model — its funding sources (foundations, major donors, membership), its editorial independence risks and how the best organizations manage them, and the evidence on whether it can achieve the scale needed to replace commercial local news, the reader revenue model (subscriptions, membership, pay-per-view) and the specific editorial and audience development strategies that make it work in local markets, the public media model and its application outside traditional public broadcasting, the cooperatively owned newsroom model, how successful local news startups have used specific editorial niches (education, criminal justice, environment) to build loyal paying audiences, the role of journalism schools and university-affiliated newsrooms, and the policy interventions — tax credits, postal subsidies, local journalism investment funds — that have been proposed or implemented and the evidence on their effectiveness.","tags":["local-news","sustainability","business","future"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T18:15:00Z","featured":true}

];
  __exports.journalismPrompts = journalismPrompts;
})();
